// Cluster configuration for a matmul testbench system.
//
// M=8, N=2, K=32  ->  Bc = M*meshRow = 128, Br = N*meshCol = 32, d = K*tileSize = 128,
// at mesh 16x4x16. The TILE is unchanged; only how it maps onto the larger mesh is.
//
// Bc and d grow TOGETHER on purpose. The GEMM does 2*Bc*Br*d MACs per KV tile while the SIMD
// does ~5*Bc*Br element-ops, so the work ratio is proportional to d: growing Bc alone leaves it
// flat, and growing d alone breaks the kernel, because d == Bc is exactly what makes
// S^T = K.Q^T and O^T = V^T.P^T the same shape and lets ONE streamer config drive both matmuls.
// Tied, doubling them doubles the GEMM:SIMD ratio (25.6 -> 51) and amortises the fixed ~382
// cycles/tile of configuration over twice the work.
//
// M and N are swapped relative to the untransposed form because FlashAttention here is
// computed TRANSPOSED:
// S^T = (Q.K^T)^T = K.Q^T is the same GEMM with its operands swapped, so
// D = [M*meshRow, N*meshCol] = [64, 32] = [Bc, Br]. That orientation is what
// lets StreamReduce's LANEWISE mode do rowmax with no horizontal fold -- one
// lane is one query row, so the reduction runs along beats. See snax-flashattn.c.
{
  // gemm configurations
  transposed_A: 0,
  transposed_B: 0,
  K: 32,
  N: 2,
  M: 8,
  bypassSIMD: 1,
  // broadcast_C won't work in the serial C input
  broadcast_C: 0,
  channel_en_C: 1,
}
